          { title: "Detailed explanation", content: "MCP stands for Model Context Protocol.\n\nIt standardizes how AI applications interact with external tools and resources.\n\nThink of MCP as a protocol boundary between:\n\n```text\nAI application\n    |\n    v\nstandardized MCP interface\n    |\n    +---- tools\n    +---- resources\n    +---- prompts\n    |\n    v\nexternal system\n```\nSpring AI supports both consuming MCP servers and exposing Spring services\nthrough MCP." },
          { title: "Worked example", content: "Suppose an assistant must answer, \"What is the status of order 4812?\" For **What MCP is**, the model can request an application-owned operation with the order identifier. The server validates the identifier, authorization, and tool arguments before executing the operation, then returns a bounded result to the model." },
          { title: "Practical use", content: "In a real Spring AI project, **What MCP is** should be introduced only after the requirement and access pattern are clear. Start with the smallest working configuration, make provider and model assumptions explicit, then add validation, error handling, and observability before treating the feature as production-ready." },
          { title: "Deep mental model", content: "Think of **What MCP is** as a capability boundary, not as permission for the model to run arbitrary code. The model proposes an action; application code decides whether that action is valid and allowed." },
          { title: "Interview focus", content: "An interviewer may ask: **What is What MCP is, why would you use it, and what trade-off does it introduce?** A strong answer should define the concept, show a small Spring AI example, explain where it sits in the request flow, mention one failure mode, and compare it with the closest alternative. Avoid claiming that Spring AI or a model guarantees behavior that actually depends on the selected provider, model, configuration, or application code." },
          { title: "Common pitfalls", content: "Common pitfalls for **What MCP is** include: Treating model-generated arguments as trusted input; exposing destructive operations without authorization; giving tools excessive scope; leaking secrets through tool results; and assuming tool calling is identical across models." },
          { title: "When to use / avoid", content: "Use **What MCP is** when the model needs a controlled application capability. Avoid exposing a tool when the same result can be computed deterministically in application code, or when the operation is too dangerous to delegate without a stronger approval boundary." },
          { title: "Production scenario", content: "In a production Spring AI service, **What MCP is** should have an explicit owner, configuration boundary, timeout/failure policy, observability signal, and security review. Test normal traffic as well as malformed input, provider errors, slow responses, retries, and unexpected model output. Keep secrets outside source control and avoid exposing internal prompts, credentials, or private customer data through logs or model context." },
          { title: "Related concepts", content: "Related concepts: ChatClient, ToolCallback, @Tool, @ToolParam, tool execution, authorization, input validation, MCP tools, observability." }
